    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, enabling IPv6 on an interface is done by configuring the `inet6` family under the interface's unit\\n- The Cisco `ipv6 enable` command is translated to Juniper's `family inet6` configuration\\n- Since the original configuration had IPv6 enabled on both GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and GigabitEthernet2/0/0, both interfaces need the equivalent Juniper configuration\\n- Juniper interface naming uses `ge` prefix instead of `GigabitEthernet`\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, enabling IPv6 on an interface is done by configuring the `inet6` family under the interface's unit\\n- The Cisco `ipv6 enable` command is translated to Juniper's `family inet6` configuration\\n- Since the original configuration had IPv6 enabled on both GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and GigabitEthernet2/0/0, both interfaces need the equivalent Juniper configuration\\n- Juniper interface naming uses `ge` prefix instead of `GigabitEthernet`\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
